1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,142
If you became a mother in nineteen

2
00:00:02,142 --> 00:00:04,725
fifty-five, you were probably very young.

3
00:00:04,905 --> 00:00:07,817
The typical American woman married for the

4
00:00:07,817 --> 00:00:09,690
first time at about twenty,

5
00:00:09,690 --> 00:00:11,701
and had her first baby a year

6
00:00:11,701 --> 00:00:12,602
or two later.

7
00:00:12,782 --> 00:00:14,822
You might have been twenty-one,

8
00:00:14,822 --> 00:00:16,006
holding a newborn,

9
00:00:16,006 --> 00:00:17,387
in a small new house,

10
00:00:17,387 --> 00:00:19,493
with a husband who left for work

11
00:00:19,493 --> 00:00:21,072
at seven in the morning.

12
00:00:21,252 --> 00:00:23,408
And you were expected to know exactly

13
00:00:23,408 --> 00:00:24,050
what to do.

14
00:00:24,399 --> 00:00:27,023
You were also part of something enormous.

15
00:00:27,203 --> 00:00:28,998
In nineteen fifty-five,

16
00:00:28,998 --> 00:00:31,964
about four million babies were born in

17
00:00:31,964 --> 00:00:33,368
the United States.

18
00:00:33,548 --> 00:00:35,150
Maternity wards were full.

19
00:00:35,330 --> 00:00:38,120
New schools were being planned for children

20
00:00:38,120 --> 00:00:39,742
who hadn't been born yet.

21
00:00:39,922 --> 00:00:41,901
You were one of millions of young

22
00:00:41,901 --> 00:00:43,579
women raising the baby boom.

23
00:00:43,930 --> 00:00:46,536
Today, let's follow a young mother through

24
00:00:46,536 --> 00:00:47,529
that first year.

25
00:00:47,709 --> 00:00:50,160
From the hospital to the nursery,

26
00:00:50,160 --> 00:00:53,725
the kitchen, the backyard and the pediatrician's

27
00:00:53,725 --> 00:00:54,245
office.

28
00:00:54,425 --> 00:00:56,236
If you were a mother in the

29
00:00:56,236 --> 00:00:58,114
fifties, or a baby with one,

30
00:00:58,114 --> 00:01:00,127
I think much of this will feel

31
00:01:00,127 --> 00:01:01,066
very familiar.

32
00:01:01,416 --> 00:01:03,278
Just a generation earlier,

33
00:01:03,278 --> 00:01:06,071
most American babies were born at home.

34
00:01:06,251 --> 00:01:07,978
By nineteen fifty-five,

35
00:01:07,978 --> 00:01:10,605
almost all were born in a hospital.

36
00:01:10,785 --> 00:01:13,711
Women went into the hospital as soon

37
00:01:13,711 --> 00:01:14,930
as labor began.

38
00:01:15,110 --> 00:01:16,865
Husbands drove them there,

39
00:01:16,865 --> 00:01:19,025
kissed them goodbye at the door,

40
00:01:19,025 --> 00:01:21,321
and then went to the waiting room.

41
00:01:21,501 --> 00:01:24,064
Fathers were almost never allowed in the

42
00:01:24,064 --> 00:01:24,961
delivery room.

43
00:01:25,311 --> 00:01:26,864
In the waiting room,

44
00:01:26,864 --> 00:01:28,263
the fathers paced,

45
00:01:28,263 --> 00:01:30,593
smoked and read old magazines.

46
00:01:30,773 --> 00:01:32,391
When the baby arrived,

47
00:01:32,391 --> 00:01:34,744
a nurse came out to announce it.

48
00:01:34,924 --> 00:01:37,397
Then the new father handed out cigars

49
00:01:37,397 --> 00:01:38,800
to everyone in sight,

50
00:01:38,800 --> 00:01:40,939
often with a band that said It's

51
00:01:40,939 --> 00:01:42,342
a Boy or It's a Girl.

52
00:01:42,522 --> 00:01:45,158
It was a ritual all over America.

53
00:01:45,508 --> 00:01:48,777
Many mothers were given heavy medication during

54
00:01:48,777 --> 00:01:51,421
labor, a method called twilight sleep,

55
00:01:51,421 --> 00:01:54,274
and some remembered almost nothing of the

56
00:01:54,274 --> 00:01:54,691
birth.

57
00:01:54,871 --> 00:01:58,386
Afterward, mothers usually stayed in the hospital

58
00:01:58,386 --> 00:02:00,107
for five days to a week.

59
00:02:00,287 --> 00:02:02,478
The babies stayed in a nursery behind

60
00:02:02,478 --> 00:02:03,603
a big glass window,

61
00:02:03,603 --> 00:02:05,735
and were brought to their mothers on

62
00:02:05,735 --> 00:02:06,800
a strict schedule.

63
00:02:07,151 --> 00:02:09,734
Family and friends came to the window

64
00:02:09,734 --> 00:02:11,130
to look at the baby,

65
00:02:11,130 --> 00:02:12,526
pointing and waving.

66
00:02:12,706 --> 00:02:15,420
Grandmothers argued about whose side of the

67
00:02:15,420 --> 00:02:17,187
family the baby looked like.

68
00:02:17,367 --> 00:02:18,621
And the hospital bill?

69
00:02:18,801 --> 00:02:21,083
For a normal delivery and a week's

70
00:02:21,083 --> 00:02:23,364
stay, it might be around a hundred

71
00:02:23,364 --> 00:02:24,907
to two hundred dollars,

72
00:02:24,907 --> 00:02:27,927
often partly paid by the husband's insurance.

73
00:02:28,277 --> 00:02:29,356
Then you went home.

74
00:02:29,536 --> 00:02:32,036
And home, for many young mothers in

75
00:02:32,036 --> 00:02:35,250
nineteen fifty-five, was a brand-new house in

76
00:02:35,250 --> 00:02:36,607
a brand-new suburb,

77
00:02:36,607 --> 00:02:39,393
far from their own mothers and sisters.

78
00:02:39,573 --> 00:02:42,713
Their husbands left early and came home

79
00:02:42,713 --> 00:02:43,277
at six.

80
00:02:43,457 --> 00:02:44,860
For the first time,

81
00:02:44,860 --> 00:02:47,444
a young woman might spend long days

82
00:02:47,444 --> 00:02:48,773
alone with a baby,

83
00:02:48,773 --> 00:02:50,841
with no one to ask for help.

84
00:02:51,191 --> 00:02:52,642
But there were neighbors,

85
00:02:52,642 --> 00:02:54,325
and they mattered enormously.

86
00:02:54,505 --> 00:02:57,006
On streets full of young families,

87
00:02:57,006 --> 00:02:59,654
mothers leaned over backyard fences,

88
00:02:59,654 --> 00:03:02,523
shared coffee in each other's kitchens,

89
00:03:02,523 --> 00:03:05,244
and passed baby clothes from house to

90
00:03:05,244 --> 00:03:05,686
house.

91
00:03:05,866 --> 00:03:07,712
They watched each other's children.

92
00:03:07,892 --> 00:03:09,063
For many women,

93
00:03:09,063 --> 00:03:12,187
those neighbors became lifelong friends.

94
00:03:12,538 --> 00:03:13,803
And there was Dr.

95
00:03:13,983 --> 00:03:14,703
Spock.

96
00:03:14,883 --> 00:03:16,639
Benjamin Spock's book,

97
00:03:16,639 --> 00:03:19,273
The Common Sense Book of Baby and

98
00:03:19,273 --> 00:03:23,265
Child Care, first published in nineteen forty-six,

99
00:03:23,265 --> 00:03:26,458
was on almost every mother's nightstand.

100
00:03:26,638 --> 00:03:28,995
It sold millions of copies.

101
00:03:29,175 --> 00:03:31,424
Its famous first line told mothers,

102
00:03:31,424 --> 00:03:33,866
trust yourself, you know more than you

103
00:03:33,866 --> 00:03:34,701
think you do.

104
00:03:34,881 --> 00:03:36,936
For a frightened young mother,

105
00:03:36,936 --> 00:03:38,921
those words meant everything.

106
00:03:39,272 --> 00:03:40,827
Then there was the nursery.

107
00:03:41,007 --> 00:03:44,326
Many young couples painted a small bedroom

108
00:03:44,326 --> 00:03:46,301
pale yellow or soft blue.

109
00:03:46,481 --> 00:03:48,089
There was a wooden crib,

110
00:03:48,089 --> 00:03:49,495
often a hand-me-down,

111
00:03:49,495 --> 00:03:51,973
a changing table and a rocking chair.

112
00:03:52,153 --> 00:03:53,929
A mobile hung over the crib.

113
00:03:54,109 --> 00:03:55,552
And on the wall,

114
00:03:55,552 --> 00:03:58,078
maybe a picture of a lamb or

115
00:03:58,078 --> 00:03:59,972
a little Bible verse.

116
00:04:00,152 --> 00:04:01,083
It was simple,

117
00:04:01,083 --> 00:04:02,811
but it was made with love.

118
00:04:03,160 --> 00:04:06,197
Babies in the fifties wore cloth diapers,

119
00:04:06,197 --> 00:04:09,233
folded and fastened with big safety pins.

120
00:04:09,413 --> 00:04:11,995
Every day, the dirty ones went into

121
00:04:11,995 --> 00:04:14,430
a pail, and then into the washing

122
00:04:14,430 --> 00:04:17,307
machine, and then onto the clothesline.

123
00:04:17,487 --> 00:04:19,862
A young mother might wash and hang

124
00:04:19,862 --> 00:04:21,609
dozens of diapers a week.

125
00:04:21,789 --> 00:04:25,008
Diaper services, which picked up dirty diapers

126
00:04:25,008 --> 00:04:26,758
and delivered clean ones,

127
00:04:26,758 --> 00:04:29,138
were a luxury many families loved.

128
00:04:29,487 --> 00:04:31,507
Disposable diapers existed,

129
00:04:31,507 --> 00:04:34,199
but they were expensive and not very

130
00:04:34,199 --> 00:04:34,573
good.

131
00:04:34,753 --> 00:04:37,949
Pampers wouldn't arrive in stores until the

132
00:04:37,949 --> 00:04:38,990
early sixties.

133
00:04:39,170 --> 00:04:41,500
Over the cloth diaper went a pair

134
00:04:41,500 --> 00:04:42,630
of rubber pants.

135
00:04:42,810 --> 00:04:45,811
And many mothers began toilet training very

136
00:04:45,811 --> 00:04:49,300
early, sometimes before the baby's first birthday.

137
00:04:49,649 --> 00:04:51,228
Feeding was different too.

138
00:04:51,408 --> 00:04:52,314
In the fifties,

139
00:04:52,314 --> 00:04:54,729
breastfeeding had fallen out of fashion.

140
00:04:54,909 --> 00:04:56,185
By the late fifties,

141
00:04:56,185 --> 00:04:58,290
only about one in four babies was

142
00:04:58,290 --> 00:05:00,714
breastfed when they left the hospital.

143
00:05:00,894 --> 00:05:03,103
Most mothers used formula,

144
00:05:03,103 --> 00:05:06,842
often a homemade mixture of evaporated milk,

145
00:05:06,842 --> 00:05:08,627
corn syrup and water,

146
00:05:08,627 --> 00:05:12,026
measured out and boiled in glass bottles

147
00:05:12,026 --> 00:05:13,131
on the stove.

148
00:05:13,480 --> 00:05:16,847
Doctors also recommended feeding babies on a

149
00:05:16,847 --> 00:05:19,448
strict schedule, every four hours,

150
00:05:19,448 --> 00:05:22,049
whether they seemed hungry or not.

151
00:05:22,229 --> 00:05:25,197
Babies started on cereal very early,

152
00:05:25,197 --> 00:05:27,093
sometimes within weeks.

153
00:05:27,273 --> 00:05:29,696
And jars of Gerber baby food were

154
00:05:29,696 --> 00:05:30,871
a modern wonder,

155
00:05:30,871 --> 00:05:33,220
lined up on the shelves of every

156
00:05:33,220 --> 00:05:35,349
supermarket in strained peas,

157
00:05:35,349 --> 00:05:37,037
carrots and applesauce.

158
00:05:37,387 --> 00:05:39,216
Babies slept on their stomachs,

159
00:05:39,216 --> 00:05:41,103
which was the advice of the day.

160
00:05:41,283 --> 00:05:43,626
They were bundled in flannel and wrapped

161
00:05:43,626 --> 00:05:46,496
in hand-knitted blankets from their grandmothers.

162
00:05:46,676 --> 00:05:48,088
And when it was warm,

163
00:05:48,088 --> 00:05:50,577
mothers often put the baby outside in

164
00:05:50,577 --> 00:05:52,528
the carriage for a nap in the

165
00:05:52,528 --> 00:05:53,200
fresh air.

166
00:05:53,380 --> 00:05:55,602
It was believed to make them strong

167
00:05:55,602 --> 00:05:56,364
and healthy.

168
00:05:56,714 --> 00:06:00,242
Every mother in nineteen fifty-five carried one

169
00:06:00,242 --> 00:06:00,917
big fear.

170
00:06:01,097 --> 00:06:02,154
Polio.

171
00:06:02,334 --> 00:06:06,219
Every summer, outbreaks swept through American towns,

172
00:06:06,219 --> 00:06:08,417
and it mostly struck children.

173
00:06:08,597 --> 00:06:11,523
Mothers kept their kids away from swimming

174
00:06:11,523 --> 00:06:13,265
pools and crowded places.

175
00:06:13,445 --> 00:06:15,510
A child with a stiff neck or

176
00:06:15,510 --> 00:06:18,091
a fever could make a mother's heart

177
00:06:18,091 --> 00:06:18,460
stop.

178
00:06:18,810 --> 00:06:20,859
Then, on April twelfth,

179
00:06:20,859 --> 00:06:23,977
nineteen fifty-five, the news came.

180
00:06:24,157 --> 00:06:27,094
Jonas Salk's polio vaccine had been tested

181
00:06:27,094 --> 00:06:29,331
on more than a million children,

182
00:06:29,331 --> 00:06:30,310
and it worked.

183
00:06:30,490 --> 00:06:31,732
Church bells rang.

184
00:06:31,912 --> 00:06:33,782
People cried in the streets.

185
00:06:33,962 --> 00:06:36,208
Mothers lined up with their children at

186
00:06:36,208 --> 00:06:38,339
schools and clinics to get the shots.

187
00:06:38,688 --> 00:06:40,514
For a mother with a new baby

188
00:06:40,514 --> 00:06:42,926
that year, it was an enormous relief.

189
00:06:43,106 --> 00:06:45,285
Her child might grow up without the

190
00:06:45,285 --> 00:06:47,588
fear she had lived with every summer.

191
00:06:47,768 --> 00:06:49,188
Within a few years,

192
00:06:49,188 --> 00:06:52,103
polio cases in America dropped by about

193
00:06:52,103 --> 00:06:53,224
ninety percent.

194
00:06:53,404 --> 00:06:55,533
Few things in the fifties made mothers

195
00:06:55,533 --> 00:06:55,982
happier.

196
00:06:56,331 --> 00:06:58,549
Doctors were very different then too.

197
00:06:58,729 --> 00:07:02,343
The family pediatrician was treated almost like

198
00:07:02,343 --> 00:07:02,804
a god.

199
00:07:02,984 --> 00:07:05,271
Mothers rarely questioned his advice.

200
00:07:05,451 --> 00:07:07,500
He might come to the house when

201
00:07:07,500 --> 00:07:08,557
a baby was sick,

202
00:07:08,557 --> 00:07:09,944
carrying a black bag.

203
00:07:10,124 --> 00:07:12,018
And a visit to his office often

204
00:07:12,018 --> 00:07:13,361
ended with a lollipop,

205
00:07:13,361 --> 00:07:15,438
and a weight and height written on

206
00:07:15,438 --> 00:07:17,392
a little card for the baby book.

207
00:07:17,742 --> 00:07:19,634
The baby was only part of the

208
00:07:19,634 --> 00:07:19,960
work.

209
00:07:20,140 --> 00:07:21,918
In nineteen fifty-five,

210
00:07:21,918 --> 00:07:24,546
a mother was also expected to keep

211
00:07:24,546 --> 00:07:25,782
a perfect house.

212
00:07:25,962 --> 00:07:27,642
Floors swept and mopped,

213
00:07:27,642 --> 00:07:30,232
beds made, laundry washed and ironed,

214
00:07:30,232 --> 00:07:32,472
dishes done, and a hot dinner on

215
00:07:32,472 --> 00:07:34,992
the table when her husband walked in

216
00:07:34,992 --> 00:07:35,622
the door.

217
00:07:35,802 --> 00:07:38,788
Studies at the time found many housewives

218
00:07:38,788 --> 00:07:41,410
worked more than fifty hours a week.

219
00:07:41,759 --> 00:07:43,745
Ironing alone took hours.

220
00:07:43,925 --> 00:07:46,056
Shirts, dresses, sheets,

221
00:07:46,056 --> 00:07:50,229
pillowcases and even handkerchiefs were ironed.

222
00:07:50,409 --> 00:07:53,787
Before permanent press fabric arrived in the

223
00:07:53,787 --> 00:07:56,551
sixties, nearly everything wrinkled.

224
00:07:56,731 --> 00:07:59,490
Many women sprinkled clothes with water,

225
00:07:59,490 --> 00:08:00,524
rolled them up,

226
00:08:00,524 --> 00:08:03,352
and stored them in the refrigerator until

227
00:08:03,352 --> 00:08:04,869
they had time to iron.

228
00:08:05,219 --> 00:08:06,857
There were new machines to help.

229
00:08:07,037 --> 00:08:09,173
Automatic washing machines,

230
00:08:09,173 --> 00:08:12,733
electric dryers, refrigerators with freezers,

231
00:08:12,733 --> 00:08:14,316
and vacuum cleaners.

232
00:08:14,496 --> 00:08:15,965
For many young mothers,

233
00:08:15,965 --> 00:08:18,774
those machines were a real blessing compared

234
00:08:18,774 --> 00:08:21,137
with how their own mothers had lived.

235
00:08:21,317 --> 00:08:23,151
But the standards rose with them.

236
00:08:23,331 --> 00:08:26,065
The house was supposed to be spotless,

237
00:08:26,065 --> 00:08:27,000
all the time.

238
00:08:27,350 --> 00:08:29,822
And dinner was serious business.

239
00:08:30,002 --> 00:08:33,440
A mother in nineteen fifty-five might cook

240
00:08:33,440 --> 00:08:35,077
meatloaf, pot roast,

241
00:08:35,077 --> 00:08:38,023
tuna noodle casserole or pork chops,

242
00:08:38,023 --> 00:08:39,415
with a vegetable,

243
00:08:39,415 --> 00:08:41,543
potatoes and Jell-O salad.

244
00:08:41,723 --> 00:08:44,927
Cookbooks like Betty Crocker's showed her how.

245
00:08:45,107 --> 00:08:47,817
And the whole family sat down together,

246
00:08:47,817 --> 00:08:50,041
every night, usually around six.

247
00:08:50,392 --> 00:08:52,168
And then the baby book itself.

248
00:08:52,348 --> 00:08:54,674
Many mothers kept one carefully,

249
00:08:54,674 --> 00:08:56,782
writing down the first smile,

250
00:08:56,782 --> 00:08:57,945
the first tooth,

251
00:08:57,945 --> 00:09:00,417
the first word and the first step.

252
00:09:00,597 --> 00:09:02,382
They tucked in a lock of hair

253
00:09:02,382 --> 00:09:03,798
from the first haircut,

254
00:09:03,798 --> 00:09:05,276
and a hospital bracelet.

255
00:09:05,456 --> 00:09:08,078
Today, those little books are some of

256
00:09:08,078 --> 00:09:10,843
the most precious things a family owns.

257
00:09:11,193 --> 00:09:13,730
Many young mothers in the suburbs didn't

258
00:09:13,730 --> 00:09:15,886
drive, or didn't have a car during

259
00:09:15,886 --> 00:09:16,394
the day.

260
00:09:16,574 --> 00:09:18,093
The family had one car,

261
00:09:18,093 --> 00:09:20,208
and the husband took it to work.

262
00:09:20,388 --> 00:09:21,967
So mothers walked.

263
00:09:22,147 --> 00:09:24,683
They pushed big baby carriages to the

264
00:09:24,683 --> 00:09:25,985
park, to the store,

265
00:09:25,985 --> 00:09:27,836
and to each other's houses.

266
00:09:28,016 --> 00:09:30,132
A big carriage with a hood and

267
00:09:30,132 --> 00:09:32,671
large wheels was a proud possession.

268
00:09:33,021 --> 00:09:35,141
Groceries were often delivered,

269
00:09:35,141 --> 00:09:37,192
or bought once a week when the

270
00:09:37,192 --> 00:09:38,559
husband could drive.

271
00:09:38,739 --> 00:09:40,702
The milkman came to the door.

272
00:09:40,882 --> 00:09:42,527
So did the bread man,

273
00:09:42,527 --> 00:09:44,173
the Fuller Brush man,

274
00:09:44,173 --> 00:09:45,584
and the Avon lady.

275
00:09:45,764 --> 00:09:47,898
For a mother stuck at home with

276
00:09:47,898 --> 00:09:50,239
a baby, those visits were a little

277
00:09:50,239 --> 00:09:51,960
bit of company in a long,

278
00:09:51,960 --> 00:09:52,648
quiet day.

279
00:09:52,999 --> 00:09:53,946
And on Sunday,

280
00:09:53,946 --> 00:09:56,111
the whole family went to church,

281
00:09:56,111 --> 00:09:58,411
the baby dressed in a little white

282
00:09:58,411 --> 00:09:58,885
outfit.

283
00:09:59,065 --> 00:10:01,453
Grandparents came for Sunday dinner,

284
00:10:01,453 --> 00:10:03,709
or the family drove to visit them.

285
00:10:03,889 --> 00:10:06,266
Those Sundays were often the one day

286
00:10:06,266 --> 00:10:08,512
a young mother could hand the baby

287
00:10:08,512 --> 00:10:10,559
to someone else for an hour and

288
00:10:10,559 --> 00:10:10,890
rest.

289
00:10:11,240 --> 00:10:12,296
And there were cameras.

290
00:10:12,476 --> 00:10:15,146
Many fathers bought a Kodak Brownie,

291
00:10:15,146 --> 00:10:16,852
or a home movie camera,

292
00:10:16,852 --> 00:10:18,780
just to film the new baby.

293
00:10:18,960 --> 00:10:21,939
The prints came back from the drugstore

294
00:10:21,939 --> 00:10:22,932
a week later,

295
00:10:22,932 --> 00:10:25,682
small and square with white borders.

296
00:10:25,862 --> 00:10:27,058
Those home movies,

297
00:10:27,058 --> 00:10:28,520
flickering and silent,

298
00:10:28,520 --> 00:10:30,380
are now how many of us first

299
00:10:30,380 --> 00:10:32,639
saw our own mothers when they were

300
00:10:32,639 --> 00:10:33,037
young.

301
00:10:33,387 --> 00:10:35,210
It wasn't always easy.

302
00:10:35,390 --> 00:10:38,954
Magazines and television showed mothers who were

303
00:10:38,954 --> 00:10:41,997
always smiling, always calm and always in

304
00:10:41,997 --> 00:10:43,111
a pretty dress.

305
00:10:43,291 --> 00:10:44,812
Real mothers were tired.

306
00:10:44,992 --> 00:10:46,571
Many felt lonely.

307
00:10:46,751 --> 00:10:49,227
Some felt they had given up dreams

308
00:10:49,227 --> 00:10:50,902
of college or a career,

309
00:10:50,902 --> 00:10:53,305
and didn't feel they could say so

310
00:10:53,305 --> 00:10:53,961
out loud.

311
00:10:54,310 --> 00:10:55,946
In nineteen sixty-three,

312
00:10:55,946 --> 00:10:58,467
Betty Friedan wrote a book called The

313
00:10:58,467 --> 00:11:01,465
Feminine Mystique, about what she called the

314
00:11:01,465 --> 00:11:03,169
problem that has no name.

315
00:11:03,349 --> 00:11:05,780
Many women who had been young mothers

316
00:11:05,780 --> 00:11:08,211
in the fifties read it and recognized

317
00:11:08,211 --> 00:11:08,933
themselves.

318
00:11:09,113 --> 00:11:10,930
But in nineteen fifty-five,

319
00:11:10,930 --> 00:11:13,620
there were few words for those feelings,

320
00:11:13,620 --> 00:11:15,975
and most mothers simply carried on.

321
00:11:16,324 --> 00:11:18,542
There were also practical limits.

322
00:11:18,722 --> 00:11:19,745
In many states,

323
00:11:19,745 --> 00:11:22,611
a married woman had trouble getting credit

324
00:11:22,611 --> 00:11:23,703
in her own name.

325
00:11:23,883 --> 00:11:26,768
Many employers wouldn't hire a mother with

326
00:11:26,768 --> 00:11:29,448
small children, and some fired women as

327
00:11:29,448 --> 00:11:31,441
soon as they became pregnant.

328
00:11:31,621 --> 00:11:34,745
A young mother in nineteen fifty-five depended

329
00:11:34,745 --> 00:11:37,529
on her husband in ways her granddaughters

330
00:11:37,529 --> 00:11:38,819
can hardly imagine.

331
00:11:39,168 --> 00:11:41,806
And yet, many women who became mothers

332
00:11:41,806 --> 00:11:44,581
in the fifties remember those years with

333
00:11:44,581 --> 00:11:45,275
deep love.

334
00:11:45,455 --> 00:11:46,988
Their children were healthy.

335
00:11:47,168 --> 00:11:49,234
Their husbands had steady jobs.

336
00:11:49,414 --> 00:11:51,415
Their neighbors were young and full of

337
00:11:51,415 --> 00:11:51,678
hope.

338
00:11:51,858 --> 00:11:53,180
Many say those years,

339
00:11:53,180 --> 00:11:55,948
with little children running through a small

340
00:11:55,948 --> 00:11:58,464
house, were the happiest of their lives.

341
00:11:58,814 --> 00:12:01,748
Those mothers raised the largest generation in

342
00:12:01,748 --> 00:12:02,832
American history.

343
00:12:03,012 --> 00:12:04,880
They packed the lunches,

344
00:12:04,880 --> 00:12:06,360
sewed the costumes,

345
00:12:06,360 --> 00:12:07,294
ran the PTA,

346
00:12:07,294 --> 00:12:08,929
drove the Cub Scouts,

347
00:12:08,929 --> 00:12:11,577
and sat up with fevers through the

348
00:12:11,577 --> 00:12:12,044
night.

349
00:12:12,224 --> 00:12:14,279
The baby boom didn't just happen.

350
00:12:14,459 --> 00:12:17,732
Millions of young mothers made it happen,

351
00:12:17,732 --> 00:12:19,567
one long day at a time.

352
00:12:19,917 --> 00:12:21,732
If your mother had you in the

353
00:12:21,732 --> 00:12:23,923
fifties, think of her for a moment.

354
00:12:24,103 --> 00:12:25,792
Twenty-two years old,

355
00:12:25,792 --> 00:12:27,321
in a small kitchen,

356
00:12:27,321 --> 00:12:29,413
with a baby on her hip and

357
00:12:29,413 --> 00:12:30,941
a pot on the stove.

358
00:12:31,121 --> 00:12:33,468
She probably never thought she was doing

359
00:12:33,468 --> 00:12:34,465
anything special.

360
00:12:34,645 --> 00:12:35,620
But she was.

361
00:12:35,970 --> 00:12:37,909
So tell us in the comments.

362
00:12:38,089 --> 00:12:39,897
If you were a young mother in

363
00:12:39,897 --> 00:12:42,327
the fifties, what do you remember most?

364
00:12:42,507 --> 00:12:44,614
Or tell us about your own mother,

365
00:12:44,614 --> 00:12:46,721
and what she did for your family.

366
00:12:46,901 --> 00:12:48,225
We read every comment.

367
00:12:48,405 --> 00:12:51,012
Next time, we're asking what happened to

368
00:12:51,012 --> 00:12:52,968
the American drive-in theater.

369
00:12:53,148 --> 00:12:54,855
Thank you for spending this time with

370
00:12:54,855 --> 00:12:54,994
me.
